ation because developers reconstruct them from behavior (tests, logs) rather than specification, and behavior-preserving reconstruction always overfits to accidental invariants, guaranteeing that each reimplementation carries forward strictly more hidden constraints than the prior version.", "probability": 0.03}, {"conjecture": "Monads and effect types can encode specific categories of implicit invariants, but the majority of accumulated implicit invariants are resource-lifetime and partial-order constraints that are provably inexpressible in any compositional type system, making their growth fundamentally unaddressable by type-level abstraction.", "probability": 0.02}, {"conjecture": "Implicit invariant density (hidden constraints per line of code) increases over time because developers add behavior to existing functions rather than creating new ones, and each addition constrains the function's future callers in ways invisible to the call-site type context.", "probability": 0.08}, {"conjecture": "The half-life of an implicit invariant exceeds the tenure of the developers who created it, so at any given time the majority of active implicit invariants have no living author, making their discovery require archaeology rather than reasoning and ensuring that change cost grows monotonically regardless of team skill.", "probability": 0.07}]}
